WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:12.600
Hi, everyone. Thank you for joining us for our opening webinar for Imaging Science

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:18.160
program. This program is a celebration of science photography and making

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.789
observations of the world around us through photography.

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:27.060
So through this webinar, photography tutorials, we're going to be and other

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.000
social media posts, we're going to be giving you the tools that you need to

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:33.920
take some really interesting observations of the world around you and

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.580
share them with us. And with that, I'm going to head. I'm going to hand this off

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:46.540
to Erika Reinfeld, who is the the Outreach Coordinator at the Koch Institute. So

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:53.410
take it away, Erika. [Erika] Thank you so much, Brian. All right, let me pull my screen up

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:59.380
here. And thank you to everyone who's filled out the poll. We'll be looking at

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:04.899
the results of that in just a little bit. But let me...I'm going to start things off

00:01:04.899 --> 00:01:09.009
with an intro and then we'll jump into our conversation with our expert

00:01:09.009 --> 00:01:11.920
photographers. Alright, so hopefully everyone can see this now,

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:17.650
And before I move into introducing our speakers, I just want to tell you a

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:22.990
little bit about who I am and why I'm here and I'm gonna go. I'm gonna move the

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:28.930
poll off the screen and let you end that Brian. If you are in control of that.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:34.600
Alright, so I am a scientist by training. I'm an educator in practice. And as you

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:41.710
can see most of my career has been spent looking at interdisciplinary work. Alright so hi

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:46.360
this is me. This is me in our public galleries here at the Koch Institute at

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:56.409
MIT. And that's actually what I want to talk to you about first. This is the, this

00:01:56.409 --> 00:02:00.610
is I think the main reason Brian asked me to be a part of this webinar. And so,

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:05.500
as part of my role I manage the public galleries at the Koch Institute, which is

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.280
MIT's Cancer Research Center. It's a convergence of biology and engineering.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:15.099
And one of the central exhibits in our public galleries is the image awards

00:02:15.099 --> 00:02:21.180
exhibition. So that's every year, scientists and researchers from MIT's

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:26.050
Biomedical Engineering life sciences submit images and a

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:31.300
panel of judges, who are made up of scientists and artists and others, choose 10 images

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:36.730
from we get about 150 every year and they select 10 of those images to be put

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:42.670
on display in these eight foot tall light boxes on Main Street in Cambridge. So

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:47.050
what you see here is a snapshot from our 2016 exhibition. There's a cryogenically

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:52.480
frozen stem cell on the left and then where Asha's standing. This is a

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:56.520
mosaic of synthetic polymers that are interacting with various cell types

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:02.920
taken with fluorescence microscopy. So any image in the display has to meet two

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.000
key criteria, it has to be visually engaging and it has to be scientifically

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.630
compelling. It can't just be a pretty picture. It has to tell a good story

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:16.630
about real research. So in Asha's case, she was examining these interactions between

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:21.580
synthetic materials and cells to identify promising candidates for new

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:26.620
materials to be used in biomedical research and clinical applications.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:30.550
So it's a screening experiment which serves as a bridge between discovery and

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:35.380
innovation, ideas and actions, and that's one of the themes I think we want to

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:40.330
talk about in our exploration of science photography that connection between

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:44.770
ideas and action. But before we do that I want to show you another view of this

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:50.170
image, which is juxtaposed with images of exploding stars taken by the Chandra

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:55.870
X-ray observatory. This was put together by a former colleague of mine, Kim Arcand

00:03:55.870 --> 00:04:00.940
and I think it's just a really beautiful illustration of how scientific imagery

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:06.580
can be used to make connections between different disciplines. So on that note, if

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:11.830
if you, if you were reading my earlier slide you'll see that I spent a fair bit of

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:16.030
time in the museum world looking at how people make sense of scientific images;

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:21.000
do they see them as information or do they see them as art and well worth a

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.520
graduate degree, the answer, of course, is yes, they do. And so, to illustrate that I

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:31.120
have one more comparison that I want to show you. So this is, the image on the

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.140
left was taken by the Hubble Space Telescope to commemorate the 15th

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:39.340
anniversary of the satellite's launch. It shows a star forming nebula in the

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:41.949
constellation Serpens and then on the right is a

00:04:41.949 --> 00:04:45.789
painting, I admit it, It's not a photograph, by Thomas Moran.

00:04:45.789 --> 00:04:50.589
Elizabeth Kessler from Stanford has pointed out how the decisions that the

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:56.199
scientists made in the colors and the framing of images taken with cameras on

00:04:56.199 --> 00:05:02.080
the Hubble Space Telescope really invoke these 19th century paintings and

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.490
photographs as well in both their composition and theme. So this kind of

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:10.210
blue sky background and the towering pillars and the light reflecting around

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:14.289
them. It represents scientific knowledge about the nebula where the stars are

00:05:14.289 --> 00:05:20.289
being born, but they also call to mind this exploration of new frontiers. So,

00:05:20.289 --> 00:05:25.479
with that connection between art and science in mind, I'm going to set aside the

00:05:25.479 --> 00:05:30.849
fancy telescopes and microscopes and leave you with some more down to earth

00:05:30.849 --> 00:05:33.580
pictures as I introduce our first speaker.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:38.110
I'll introduce Felice Frankel who is a research scientist in the departments

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:43.659
of chemical engineering and mechanical engineering here, or there, at MIT. She's

00:05:43.659 --> 00:05:47.469
an award winning science photographer and her most recent book is "Picturing

00:05:47.469 --> 00:05:50.409
Science and Engineering" and it's published by MIT Press.

00:05:50.409 --> 00:05:54.849
She teaches an online edX course, Making Science and Engineering Pictures, A

00:05:54.849 --> 00:05:58.599
Practical Guide to Presenting Your Work. I've known Felice for a number of years

00:05:58.599 --> 00:06:03.879
now, we've worked on both education workshops and on image and on this image

00:06:03.879 --> 00:06:07.060
awards project. And so, I am delighted that she's going to be our first speaker

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:14.740
and share her experience as a science photographer. [Felice] Wonderful, can, can, can

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:18.399
you hear me. [laughs] It's wonderful to be here.

00:06:18.399 --> 00:06:26.319
I want to thank Erika and and Brian for inviting me. I'm really delighted to do

00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:30.939
this on a beautiful day like today and wrap our head around something that is

00:06:30.939 --> 00:06:36.430
beautiful and something that is somewhat of a distraction if I could call it that,

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:42.370
but it's it's a real joy to be with all of you. And thank you for coming. I am now

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:52.870
I am going to share. I hope play my screen. And thank you, Erika and Brian for inviting

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:56.140
me. I'm so delighted and I can't wait to talk to

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:59.680
Keith and look at his amazing pictures which we're all going to be

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:05.800
astounded by. But my whole thing is going to try to explain to you why I do what

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:10.030
I'm doing. Goethe wrote, "that which we know . . . we have

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:17.920
first seen." Well, let me tell you, when I make photographs I see things that I've

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:22.900
never seen before, obviously. But I also see that it is a

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:28.330
means of discovery. I'm constantly asking questions whenever I take a picture of

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:33.910
some sort of scientific phenomenon. This is an old picture that I did not take

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:39.040
from many years ago, this kind of started me in my whole new world taken out of

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:46.330
from a lab that George Whitesides ran it at Harvard. It's a little complicated, but

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:52.180
I'll quickly tell you that it is water that is spreading out on a surface and

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:57.940
stopping at what we're calling hydrophobic lines. That's what started me

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:02.410
on my whole new journey because that's a picture that they made. And I said to

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:07.620
them, we could do better. And we did. This is the same phenomena where the

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:14.410
hydrophobic lines are preventing the water from blending together. That is in

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:20.229
fact what the science is about. This was my job, to help them communicate the

00:08:20.229 --> 00:08:26.410
science better. This is Alice Nasto's image of material that she made that

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:33.339
she's studying in mechanical engineering. It's a fine image. This is the image that

00:08:33.339 --> 00:08:38.620
I made of the identical material. I hope you see the difference. If you don't see

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:42.430
the difference, I'm in real trouble. But basically what I'm trying to do is

00:08:42.430 --> 00:08:49.180
engage you to look more clearly at the at the research. Here's an image that I got off

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:55.570
of the of online and it's a picture of something called ferro fluid. This is the

00:08:55.570 --> 00:09:01.030
picture that I made many years ago of ferro fluid. That I guess people still

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:06.620
enjoy looking at. Ferro fluid is oil suspension of small iron

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:12.680
particles. And when you put magnets next to it, it responds to the magnetic field.

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:16.250
I made this for book that George Whitesides and I did called "On The

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:22.220
Surface of Things". It - for me, most of this is about getting you to see and to first

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:29.000
look to engage you and to get you to understand that making beautiful images

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:33.530
and honest images, which I could get to at the end, is a means of engagement,

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:39.560
which I think is critical at this point of our lives. We've got to engage people

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:44.630
into our what is going on in the research labs and that's why I wrote the book

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:48.890
"Picturing Science and Engineering". It's for researchers, but there's a lot of

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:54.830
photography basic photography in it and I'm unabashedly introducing you to the

00:09:54.830 --> 00:10:00.560
book. I hope you could take a look at it. You can use a flatbed scanner. It's quite

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:05.480
extraordinary what you could do with a flatbed scanner at the end if you set the

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:14.090
DPI correctly. We I talked about using backgrounds that kind of inform what it

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:20.930
is that we're looking at. I show a lot of microscopic images and how using

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:25.340
certain techniques and microscopy could advance the way it looks. And also,

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:31.010
frankly, getting you to look. That's what I'm trying to do. And the other part of

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:35.660
the book is all about the fact that science, in fact, is everywhere and I

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:41.360
suggest that when you make pictures of everything around you, it's a means of

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:47.900
getting you to ask questions. What is it, what is it that exactly I'm seeing? If

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:52.490
we had if we had more interactivity, I would ask you what it is that you think you're

00:10:52.490 --> 00:11:00.200
looking at. And what you are looking at is the glass cover of a sautéing pan, and

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:06.890
I'm sautéing different peppers. The thing about this example is that I made it

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:11.990
with my phone and years ago, I used to tell the scientists, you really can't

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:15.650
take great pictures with your phone. Well, I've changed my mind.

00:11:15.650 --> 00:11:20.420
I,  it's quite remarkable. Even walking down Comm Ave. I

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:25.399
remember distinctly seeing these trees for whatever reason they were wrapped

00:11:25.399 --> 00:11:32.300
around with cellophane and I was looking at these assemblies of drops around the

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:39.730
crease. There is a scientific reason for the reason why that they are lining up.

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:46.250
Everywhere is science, and why not start making pictures of it. This is just more

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:52.490
fun. I got this device where I put my phone on the microscope. It was

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:57.589
it it was it was a device that easily puts it, allows you to see it. And these are just

00:11:57.589 --> 00:12:01.339
coffee bubbles that are kind of doing their thing.

00:12:01.339 --> 00:12:06.529
I'm also very involved with finding the metaphor. Sometimes you actually cannot

00:12:06.529 --> 00:12:14.290
always take images of what the science is so trying to find some sort of

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:18.620
photographic metaphor. In this situation we're trying to describe what

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:23.540
binary means, this is the the way computers talk to each other. This is the

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:31.610
inside of a music box and the notes go on off, on off, and that is a suggestion

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:37.130
of a metaphor. Here's where I am, in fact, putting a lot of pieces together of

00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:41.779
photographs to make an image that actually doesn't exist. The image exists,

00:12:41.779 --> 00:12:46.579
but the thing that you're seeing doesn't really exist. Hopefully it's telling you a

00:12:46.579 --> 00:12:52.220
story of something called remote epitaxy, which is too complicated to go into. But

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:58.000
these are all pieces of pictures that I put together. But in the end, I always

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:03.589
discuss with the researchers to maintain the scientific integrity in your images.

00:13:03.589 --> 00:13:08.149
And that is a long conversation that perhaps we'll have at some point. For

00:13:08.149 --> 00:13:13.750
example, when I made this picture of a yeast colony out of Gerry Fink's lab,

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:23.060
what I decided to do was to delete the petri dish, because I was blown away at

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:30.140
what was going on in this yeast colony. The detail was astounding, and I

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:34.100
wanted you to only pay attention to that. So I deleted

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:39.860
the petri dish. The question is, was I permitted to do so. And that is again a

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:45.730
long conversation that perhaps we might have someday. I say yes because the data

00:13:45.730 --> 00:13:53.389
was not manipulated. This is the data, not the petri dish. So there we go, there's a

00:13:53.389 --> 00:13:57.800
little hint of what I do. And it's a real joy to be with you guys.

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:03.950
I will stop sharing.
[Erika] Thank you so much Felice. Alright so what we want to do

00:14:03.950 --> 00:14:07.160
now is turn things over to Keith and he'll give you a little taste of what he

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:10.970
does. I think those of you out there will notice that we've we've chosen these two

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:15.230
folks for very particular reasons they complement each other beautifully. So

00:14:15.230 --> 00:14:19.519
we're going to hear from Keith momentarily. And then we'll then we'll have a little

00:14:19.519 --> 00:14:22.850
discussion, we'll get the two of them actually talking to each other about

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:26.000
the science photos and we'll start to take some questions from your audience

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:30.970
from you in the audience. Again, using the Q and A  feature. So let me introduce Keith.

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:35.420
Keith is a celebrated underwater photographer working at the intersection

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:39.890
of art, science and technology to showcase the visual complexity of

00:14:39.890 --> 00:14:45.800
underwater environments from coral reefs to coastal rivers to the wide open sea.

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:49.820
He is an assistant professor of photography at SUNY the Fashion

00:14:49.820 --> 00:14:53.870
Institute of Technology and a visiting artist at Massachusetts Institute of

00:14:53.870 --> 00:15:00.170
Technology Sea Grant and the recipient of the Ernest F. Hollings Ocean Awareness

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:09.950
Award. I met Keith  probably almost a year ago because one of his planktonic

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:14.540
images is currently on display in the Koch Institute public galleries, so he

00:15:14.540 --> 00:15:19.040
has a beautiful image of dinoflagellates and I got to talk to him

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:22.310
about the story behind it, but he takes a lot of wonderful pictures and I'm going to

00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:24.666
let him tell you what he does.

00:15:24.666 --> 00:15:28.339
[Keith] Oh. Thank you very much for inviting me here. Can

00:15:28.339 --> 00:15:35.420
you guys all see me and see my screen?
[Erika] Yes, good to me.
[Keith]Okay, good.
[Brian] Looks good.

00:15:35.420 --> 00:15:40.040
[Keith] So thank you, Erika and Brian. It's wonderful to be here and I have to say

00:15:40.040 --> 00:15:43.579
it's just so inspiring to follow Felice, her stunning images and the

00:15:43.579 --> 00:15:48.140
photographic process. So it's a real honor to be here, so thank you. Um, I

00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:51.589
thought I would talk today about what it's like working with conservation

00:15:51.589 --> 00:15:55.670
based organizations and being fortunate to travel around the world to use the

00:15:55.670 --> 00:16:00.350
art of photography and ocean photography, in particular, to inspire awareness in

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:05.449
conservation. This picture here is an aerial photo of tropical coral reef from

00:16:05.449 --> 00:16:10.699
about 1000 feet within the remote Fijian Islands of Totoya. The photograph

00:16:10.699 --> 00:16:13.790
was captured on board a small helicopter with the doors removed

00:16:13.790 --> 00:16:17.540
sitting outside the helicopter. I was harnessed and tethered in with the wind

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:22.190
blowing and you could uh communicating with the pilot via headsets to get the right

00:16:22.190 --> 00:16:28.700
pitch angle and image here. For the next few minutes I'd like to take you on a

00:16:28.700 --> 00:16:34.160
journey beneath the waves. Underwater photography is a challenge. We're limited

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:39.140
by air, light, time, depth, animal behavior, just to name a few of the constraints. I

00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:43.220
like this image of me diving because it a captures the strobes

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:47.540
illuminating the reef. At depth the wavelength of red is absorbed and the

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:51.290
ocean appears of blue gray, but with the addition of light, I'm able to reveal

00:16:51.290 --> 00:16:57.230
vibrant colors. In the Galapagos Islands, I was on an assignment focusing on

00:16:57.230 --> 00:17:00.650
biodiversity in sharks. As you began to swim back to the boat,

00:17:00.650 --> 00:17:04.579
I observed a large aggregation of sea stars along the vast open sandy bottom.

00:17:04.579 --> 00:17:08.360
The Galapagos is known for its bio diverse marine ecosystems. It's full of

00:17:08.360 --> 00:17:12.770
mystery and intrigue. But the colors of these sea stars are natural, but they

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:19.339
revealed by the light of the strobe. Wildlife photography is about hours, days,

00:17:19.339 --> 00:17:23.329
weeks, maybe even years of patience, followed by an instant of action. In

00:17:23.329 --> 00:17:27.410
this photograph of a juvenile frigate bird diving to catch a fish is an

00:17:27.410 --> 00:17:31.460
example of that. It was a behavior I observed and spent two days waiting in

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:35.420
shallow water with the lens pre focused, strobes above and below the water.

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:39.260
Frigate birds never land in the water. These birds are unbelievable. They

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:42.530
accelerate downward focusing on the fish when they get near the surface they

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:46.640
virtually stall. They place their head in the water, they switch between surface

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:50.180
vision underwater vision without blinking, catch the fish and then fly

00:17:50.180 --> 00:17:55.790
away all in an instant. I stopped the lens, snapped the picture, looked at back,

00:17:55.790 --> 00:18:01.520
and with a little bit of luck, I managed to get just this photo here. There're

00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:05.480
also unexpected moments. Within the tentacles of a jellyfish is a school of

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:09.950
juvenile Trevally that seek shelter. If you look closely, you can see that,

00:18:09.950 --> 00:18:13.640
you can see them right in the tentacles. To capture this image I

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:17.360
inverted myself underwater giving the fishes' perspective of what life must

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:23.180
look like through the waves into the clouds in the sky. Oftentimes I

00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:25.400
collaborate with scientists and I often experiment with

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:28.340
other ways to visualize marine environment. This photo, one of my

00:18:28.340 --> 00:18:32.750
colleagues using a fluorescent strobes to survey the coral reefs. The

00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:35.990
psychedelic colors emitted by fluorescence light showcase another way

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:40.910
of looking at art in nature. While at the same point, the colors may lead to a

00:18:40.910 --> 00:18:45.680
deeper understanding about the health of our corals. Sometimes I'm on assignments

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:47.690
in Indonesia or other places around the world

00:18:47.690 --> 00:18:51.290
diving with scientists setting out to find and discover new possible species

00:18:51.290 --> 00:18:55.160
of coral. This is one that's being described as a flower like pattern that

00:18:55.160 --> 00:19:00.380
is unlike any other known species. People often ask, "What is it like to travel

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:03.710
with you on assignment?" and actually the most stressful part of my job is so

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:06.710
nervously checking all the luggage and bringing all the equipment everywhere  I

00:19:06.710 --> 00:19:10.760
need to go. And just to give a sense of the kind of equipment that I have, these

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:14.260
are the housings that are all broken apart as they're ready to be cleaned and

00:19:14.260 --> 00:19:20.480
assembled. I'm currently working on a multi year project entitled "Space to Sea

00:19:20.480 --> 00:19:23.510
— A Photographic Journey into Stellwagen Bank National Marine Sanctuary",

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:27.530
just 25 nautical miles off the coast of Boston. It's part of a Hollings ocean

00:19:27.530 --> 00:19:31.100
awareness award to showcase the surprising diversity of marine life off

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:34.910
our coast. To accomplish these images as part of my

00:19:34.910 --> 00:19:38.270
process, I'm working with students to modify and prototype wireless camera

00:19:38.270 --> 00:19:42.380
systems that enhance image making. We're bringing students into the field to

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:46.160
experience the local marine wildlife. Our process is to conduct plankton tows.

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:49.730
Bring the living samples back to the lab to be photographed under a microscope.

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:55.340
And we're inspired by the work of scientists at MIT Sea Grant, where we're

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:59.840
experimenting with a variety of photo microscopy image making techniques. This

00:19:59.840 --> 00:20:03.680
image was created adding a drop of milk on the slide and just

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:09.340
filming the dynamic flow visualization created by this Clione or pteropod.

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:14.810
There are other cool animals that are all alien like a plankton living

00:20:14.810 --> 00:20:19.670
in the ocean. This is a larval crab. And if you look closely, you can see its

00:20:19.670 --> 00:20:23.480
compound eyes that are blue. Soon this creature will drop to the sea floor

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:29.390
bottom, sea floor bottom and become a tiny full formed crab. As a wildlife

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:32.980
photographer, I apply my image making skills to the micro scale. This

00:20:32.990 --> 00:20:37.010
composition of three Ceratium is currently on exhibit at the MIT Koch Public

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:39.170
Galleries, and once the university's back open,

00:20:39.170 --> 00:20:45.260
I encourage all of you to join and visit. On this one magical day, working with a

00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:48.740
team of scientists, naturalists, and boat captains, I slipped into the water with a

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:53.510
wetsuit, snorkel, 360 multi camera system, looking to film what we thought was a

00:20:53.510 --> 00:20:57.650
Basking Shark, moving on the surface. To my surprise, instead of encountering a

00:20:57.650 --> 00:21:01.100
Basking Shark, it was an enormous 3000 pound Great White Shark, later identified

00:21:01.100 --> 00:21:04.700
as Large Marge. I did the only thing I know how to do, which is keep the camera

00:21:04.700 --> 00:21:08.990
stable and captured 17 glorious seconds of footage from tip to tail that can be

00:21:08.990 --> 00:21:14.120
viewed in immersive virtual reality experience. Photography really has the

00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:17.780
power to change our understanding of the world around us and it is my lifelong

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:21.050
goal to use the artistry of underwater photography to transform public

00:21:21.050 --> 00:21:25.550
perception. That sparks our imagination inspires conservation and builds a local

00:21:25.550 --> 00:21:28.820
stewardship about the extraordinary underwater wildlife and to connect

00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:32.590
residents in New England with the local marine wildlife just off our shores.

